{
  "rationale": "Evidence. Iteration 4 (kp 4.0, ki 0.3, kd 0.25, base_speed 1.3) is the best raced design at 74.19, ahead of iteration 1 (71.2). Its oval steering_oscillation is 0.254, above the 0.2 concern level, while s-bend (0.128) and slalom (0.122) are well under it. max_error has sat at 0.062 to 0.065 m in every iteration whatever the gains (kp 2.5 to 4.0, kd 0.05 to 0.25, ki 0.3 to 0.9). Every run still leaves the line, with one line-loss event each. Gain changes have stopped moving that floor, so the limit looks geometric: the sensors at y = 0.04 see the line only after the wheels have already drifted.\n\nChange. Keep the best-scoring controller (kp 4.0, ki 0.3, kd 0.25, base_speed 1.3, line_loss last_direction) and move all five sensors forward from y = 0.04 to y = 0.06, keeping their x spacing and 12 mm footprint. This is one geometry change against the best raced design, so the race isolates earlier look-ahead. I did not revert to kp 3.0 and kd 0.15, because iteration 4 scored higher than iteration 1 and its oscillation spike is confined mainly to the oval.\n\nExpected result. Earlier look-ahead should lower max_error below about 0.06 m and reduce line_loss_events toward 0 on some seeds. There is a risk that the added lead raises steering_oscillation, since the signal responds earlier and kd is already at 0.25. Speed stays at 1.3, so completion within 3 s is not expected. The 1.5 m/s average needed for completion is still out of reach at this speed.\n\nHypotheses and next steps. (1) The max_error floor near 0.063 m comes from sensor look-ahead, not gain. (2) Forward sensors with kd 0.25 keep oscillation under about 0.2. If max_error falls and line_loss_events reach 0 with progress near 0.995, raise base_speed toward 1.8. If oscillation exceeds 0.2 on the oval, raise kd toward 0.3 before changing kp. If max_error is unchanged, the floor is not geometric and the next test is kp about 5 or a sensor spread change. Measurements wanted: max_error, line_loss_events, progress, steering_oscillation, control_effort, rms_error, and completion_time on every track.",
